 "build_recommendation": "(1) Config: prefer the 5-minute cache TTL for interactive sessions whose inter-call gaps stay under 5 minutes: -11.7% (Haiku) / -10.9% (Sonnet) of $, no behaviour change. Verify that the harness exposes the choice. (2) Rust: a small PreToolUse Read-dedupe reflex that tracks shown line ranges per path, invalidates them on Edit/Write, and narrows the range with updatedInput or stubs a fully re-shown Read: +$0.0025/task, +1.0% of $ [0.6, 1.4] (Haiku). (3) Do not build blind output trimming or a stop signal. If anything, run a 40-sessions-per-arm live micro-A/B of the PreToolUse-only learned policy (elision note + parked output) to settle the -0.7% vs +12.6% measurement gap.",
 "so_what": "If shipped, learned output shaping is expected to change $ per task by about -1% to +4% on Haiku and about 0 on Sonnet, with 1-2 extra re-fetch calls per session and an unmeasured pass-rate risk. I am about 75% confident that a live A/B finds < 5%. Re-read dedupe is +1.0% [0.6, 1.4], and I am about 85% confident it survives a live A/B, since the mechanism is deterministic. The 5m TTL is -11.7% with near certainty wherever it can be set. None of these hook levers reaches 15-20%."
